Subject: [PATCH] nested wayland backend: adopt the host's fractional-scale hint
 as the output scale

Quasar patch against kwin 6.7.4 (Fedora 43: kwin-6.7.4-1.fc43).
Applies on top of 0001-nested-backend-mode-ladder.patch and
0002-nested-backend-kscreen-scale.patch.

PROBLEM
-------
Quasar's per-session `ui_scale` knob (`PATCH /v1/sessions/{id}/display
{"ui_scale":1.5}`) is delivered to the nested KWin as a
`wp_fractional_scale_v1::preferred_scale` hint plus an `xdg_toplevel.configure`
at the matching, SMALLER logical size. The intended effect is "Plasma draws
bigger, the streamed resolution does not move". Nothing happened: the desktop
kept its size, `kscreen-doctor -o` kept reporting `Scale: 1`.

The host side was not the problem, and neither was delivery. Measured live on
the devbox (2026-08-19), the nested KWin's own `WAYLAND_DEBUG` shows both
events arriving together:

    wp_fractional_scale_v1#22.preferred_scale(180)          <- 1.5
    xdg_toplevel#19.configure(1707, 960, array[8])          <- 2560/1.5
    xdg_surface#18.configure(4520)                          <- ack'd

and this backend applied them: the output's mode became 2561x1440, which is
exactly `configure size * hint` from `applyConfigure()`. Only the SCALE was
back at 1 by the time anything could observe it.

The overwriter is KWin core, not this backend. `applyConfigure()` ends with
`Q_EMIT m_backend->outputsQueried()`;
`Workspace::slotOutputBackendOutputsQueried()` answers that by asking
`OutputConfigurationStore::queryConfig()` for this output's REMEMBERED
configuration and applying it - which comes straight back into
`applyChanges()` carrying the stored scale. In the session that was measured,
`~/.config/kwinoutputconfig.json` held `"scale": 1` for `WL-0`, and that is the
value that kept winning. 0002 then makes it worse in the only way it could: it
treats every `props->scale` as a deliberate user pick, so the store's echo is
latched into `m_userScale` and becomes sticky.

That is also why the KScreen slider works while the host hint does not. A
slider pick goes through `Workspace::applyOutputConfiguration()`, which ends in
`OutputConfigurationStore::storeConfig()` - so the store remembers it and
re-asserts the SAME value on every subsequent configure. The host hint was
never written there.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------
Adopts a changed host hint into the stored configuration, through the same
path a user's pick takes.

1. `handleFractionalScaleChanged()`: a hint whose VALUE changes now also sets
   `m_hostScalePending` (it already cleared `m_userScale`, 0002 hunk 3).

2. `applyConfigure()`: after the state is applied and `outputsQueried` has been
   emitted, a pending host scale schedules `adoptHostScale()` on the event loop.
   Queued deliberately: the store's own re-apply runs first and synchronously,
   from that emit, so adopting inline would be undone a moment later.

3. `adoptHostScale()` (new): applies an `OutputConfiguration` carrying only
   `scale`/`scaleSetting` via `Workspace::applyOutputConfiguration()`. That call
   ends in `storeConfig()`, so the host's value becomes the remembered one and
   survives every later configure. `applyChanges()` records it in `m_userScale`
   on the way through, which is correct: from that point it IS the configured
   scale, a later pick in Display Settings overrides it, and a later host hint
   with a new value clears it and adopts that one instead.

Precedence, extending 0002's one-liner: host hint > previous state on a hint
CHANGE; user pick > host hint until the hint changes again.

THE STREAM DOES NOT MOVE
------------------------
The change set carries `scale` and `scaleSetting` only - never a mode - so
`m_nativeSize` (the buffer/mode size) is still the host's configure size times
the host's hint, `m_parentLogicalSize` is untouched, and the root surface's
`wp_viewport` destination stays pinned to it (0001). The host sees a surface of
unchanged size and the encoded resolution is unaffected. Only the logical size
(`mode / scale`) shrinks, which is what makes Plasma draw bigger.

`Workspace::self()` is null-checked: the first configure can arrive before the
workspace exists, and the initial scale is applied through
`init()`/`applyConfigure()` anyway.

UPSTREAMABILITY
---------------
Better than 0002's, and it stands alone from the mode ladder. Upstream's nested
backend has the same latent behaviour - a host that changes its
`wp_fractional_scale_v1` hint mid-session cannot move a nested KWin's output
scale, because the store re-asserts its own - and "adopt a changed host hint
into the stored configuration" is the whole fix. It does depend on 0002's
`m_userScale` for the precedence rule, so the two would go upstream together.

diff -uNr a/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp b/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp
--- a/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp	2026-08-20 01:59:38
+++ b/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.cpp	2026-08-20 02:00:25
@@ -18,6 +18,10 @@
 #include "wayland_display.h"
 #include "wayland_layer.h"
 #include "wayland_logging.h"
+// Quasar: adopting the host's fractional-scale hint has to go through
+// Workspace::applyOutputConfiguration(), so KWin's OutputConfigurationStore
+// records it - see WaylandOutput::adoptHostScale().
+#include "workspace.h"
 
 #include <KWayland/Client/compositor.h>
 #include <KWayland/Client/pointer.h>
@@ -123,6 +127,9 @@
     // clobber the user's pick.
     if (output->m_pendingScale != scale) {
         output->m_userScale.reset();
+        // Quasar: and it has to be ADOPTED into KWin's stored output
+        // configuration, not just written into State - see adoptHostScale().
+        output->m_hostScalePending = true;
     }
     output->m_pendingScale = scale;
 }
@@ -726,6 +733,64 @@
         setState(next);
 
         Q_EMIT m_backend->outputsQueried();
+
+        // Quasar: that signal is what makes writing the scale into State above
+        // insufficient on its own. Workspace::slotOutputBackendOutputsQueried()
+        // answers it by re-applying the OutputConfigurationStore's REMEMBERED
+        // configuration for this output - scale included - which lands back in
+        // applyChanges() and overwrites the hint (kwinoutputconfig.json,
+        // "scale": 1, is the value that was winning). A host hint therefore has
+        // to be adopted INTO that stored configuration, which is what
+        // adoptHostScale() does. Queued: the store's own apply runs first,
+        // synchronously, from the emit above.
+        if (m_hostScalePending) {
+            m_hostScalePending = false;
+            QMetaObject::invokeMethod(
+                this, [this]() {
+                    adoptHostScale();
+                },
+                Qt::QueuedConnection);
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+// Quasar: make the host's wp_fractional_scale_v1 hint the output's configured
+// scale, the same way a pick in Display Settings becomes one.
+//
+// Quasar's per-session `ui_scale` knob is delivered as that hint (the host,
+// gst-wayland-display, shrinks the LOGICAL size it configures this toplevel at
+// and announces the matching preferred_scale). The point of it is "draw the
+// desktop bigger at an unchanged streamed resolution", so the hint must move
+// the output's scale, and it must survive the store re-applying its own idea of
+// the configuration on every subsequent configure.
+//
+// Going through Workspace rather than writing State directly is what achieves
+// that: applyOutputConfiguration() ends in OutputConfigurationStore::storeConfig(),
+// so the adopted value becomes the remembered one. applyChanges() then records it
+// in m_userScale, which is correct - from here on it IS the configured scale, a
+// later pick in Display Settings overrides it, and a later host hint with a NEW
+// value clears it and adopts that one (handleFractionalScaleChanged).
+//
+// The mode is untouched: only scale/scaleSetting are in the change set, so the
+// buffer keeps the host-configured pixel size and the streamed resolution does
+// not move. Only the logical size (mode / scale) shrinks.
+void WaylandOutput::adoptHostScale()
+{
+    Workspace *ws = Workspace::self();
+    if (!ws) {
+        // Too early (the first configure can arrive before the workspace exists).
+        // The initial scale is applied through init()/applyConfigure() anyway.
+        return;
+    }
+    if (m_state.scale == m_pendingScale && m_userScale.value_or(m_pendingScale) == m_pendingScale) {
+        return;
+    }
+    OutputConfiguration config;
+    const auto changeSet = config.changeSet(this);
+    changeSet->scale = m_pendingScale;
+    changeSet->scaleSetting = m_pendingScale;
+    if (ws->applyOutputConfiguration(config) != OutputConfigurationError::None) {
+        qCWarning(KWIN_WAYLAND_BACKEND) << "could not adopt the host's fractional scale" << m_pendingScale;
     }
 }
 
diff -uNr a/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.h b/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.h
--- a/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.h	2026-08-20 01:59:38
+++ b/src/backends/wayland/wayland_output.h	2026-08-20 02:01:20
@@ -151,6 +151,8 @@
     std::shared_ptr<OutputMode> modeFor(const QSize &size, bool preferred);
     void rebuildModes(State &next, const QSize &nativeSize, const QSize &currentSize);
     void refreshModeLadder();
+    // Quasar: adopt the host's fractional-scale hint as the configured scale
+    void adoptHostScale();
 
     static const wp_fractional_scale_v1_listener s_fractionalScaleListener;
     static void handleFractionalScaleChanged(void *data, struct wp_fractional_scale_v1 *wp_fractional_scale_v1, uint32_t scale120);
@@ -204,6 +206,9 @@
     std::optional<qreal> m_userScale;
     /// stable OutputMode identities, so KScreen does not see the ladder churn
     std::map<std::pair<int, int>, std::shared_ptr<OutputMode>> m_modeCache;
+    /// a host wp_fractional_scale_v1 hint changed value and has not been adopted
+    /// into KWin's stored output configuration yet - see adoptHostScale()
+    bool m_hostScalePending = false;
 };
 
 } // namespace Wayland
